The chunk reader and the streaming chunk parser in se.hirt.jfr.redact.parser
were inspired by ChunkReader and StreamingChunkParser in the OpenJDK JDK
Mission Control project, https://github.com/openjdk/jmc, available under the
Universal Permissive License v 1.0 or the BSD 3-Clause License.

Four of the test recordings in src/test/resources/recordings come from the test resources of
the OpenJDK JDK Mission Control project, https://github.com/openjdk/jmc, available
under the Universal Permissive License v 1.0:

  jrockit-r28-transitions.jfr  transitions_R28.2.jfr, with user, host and domain
                               names and IP addresses replaced using jfr-redact
                               (see ScrubPersonalData in the test sources)
  jdk9-basic.jfr               9u0.jfr, with an IP address replaced using jfr-redact
  jdk9-weblogic-medrec.jfr     wls-medrec-jdk9.jfr
  jdk21-jmc.jfr                jdk21.jfr

The test recording jdk8-guimark.jfr is guimark.jfr from the JMC tutorial,
https://github.com/thegreystone/jmc-tutorial, a recording of the GUIMark JavaFX
benchmark on JDK 8u131 on Windows, made by Marcus Hirt, who holds its copyright and
includes it here under the license of jfr-redact. Its user, host, domain and
Perforce workspace names were replaced with Alice, HOST01 and alice.host01 using
the --replace option of jfr-redact.

The test recording jmc-writer-pooled.jfr was generated for jfr-redact with the
flightrecorder.writer library of the OpenJDK JDK Mission Control project
(10.0.0-SNAPSHOT), a pure-Java JFR writer that stores thread, class and thread
group names through the java.lang.String constant pool. It contains only
synthetic data.
